Hi {{ contact.FNAME }},

Manual reporting has an obvious cost: the hours people spend exporting, reconciling, and rebuilding files.

The larger cost is what happens next:

• decisions wait for the report;
• teams debate definitions;
• margin or stock problems surface late;
• senior people become spreadsheet operators.

A useful first step is to calculate the monthly time cost of reporting. Multiply the people involved by their weekly hours, hourly cost, and 4.33 weeks.

That number does not include errors or missed decisions, but it gives the problem a baseline.

KPIBoard replaces that recurring assembly work with a governed model and automated reporting.

Hi {{ contact.FNAME }},

A dashboard is the last visible layer of analytics work.

Before it becomes reliable, someone must define the KPI, connect the systems, transform the data, govern access, build the model, validate the result, and maintain delivery.

That usually crosses five capabilities:

• analytics architecture;
• data engineering;
• BI development;
• business analysis;
• project coordination.

Hiring one specialist often moves only one part of that chain.

KPIBoard provides the complete function as a focused subscription: one priority at a time, visible progress, and ownership that remains with your business.
